      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>The Foundation of Pay Commission Architecture: The Minimum Wage Floor</h2>
        <p>
          In the administrative framework of Indian Central Pay Commissions, every horizontal cell and vertical progression step across the 18 levels of the unified Pay Matrix originates from a single benchmark: the <strong>Minimum Basic Pay (Pay Matrix Level 1)</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The minimum basic pay is not an arbitrary figure derived from fiscal surplus or discretionary budget allowances. It serves as the legal, economic, and moral foundation representing the minimum remuneration required for an unskilled entry-level employee in public service to maintain physical health, working efficiency, family security, and dignity.
        </p>
        <p>
          When the 7th Central Pay Commission replaced the historical Pay Band 1 and Grade Pay ₹1,800 system with Level 1, it fixed the starting minimum wage at <strong>₹18,000 per month</strong> (up from ₹7,000 under the 6th CPC). As retail prices for essential commodities, housing, utilities, and healthcare have advanced over the subsequent decade, fixing the revised starting wage under the <strong>8th Central Pay Commission (8th CPC)</strong> has become the primary topic of representation between employee confederations and the Ministry of Finance.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>How Minimum Pay Is Derived: The Dr. Wallace Aykroyd Formula</h2>
        <p>
          Since the 3rd Central Pay Commission, the statutory foundation for calculating minimum wages in India has followed the <strong>norms established by the 15th Indian Labour Conference (ILC) in 1957</strong>, incorporating the nutritional dietary parameters formulated by <strong>Dr. Wallace Aykroyd</strong>, former Director of the Nutrition Research Laboratories (now the National Institute of Nutrition, Hyderabad).
        </p>
        <p>
          The Aykroyd formula treats the entry-level public servant as the principal breadwinner supporting a standard household of <strong>three adult consumption units</strong> (consisting of the employee, spouse, and two dependent children treated as 0.5 units each):
        </p>

        <div class="formula-box">
          <p>
            <strong>Standard Consumption Unit Allocation:</strong> Husband (1.0 Unit) &plus; Wife (0.8 Unit) &plus; 2 Children (0.6 &plus; 0.6 = 1.2 Units) = <strong>3.0 Adult Consumption Units</strong>
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          The nutritional and physiological parameters mandated under the formula include:
        </p>
        <ul class="editorial-list">
          <li><strong>Caloric Requirement:</strong> A minimum net intake of <strong>2,700 calories per adult per day</strong> for moderate physical activity, comprising balanced allotments of cereals (475g), pulses (80g), milk (200ml), edible oils (40g), vegetables (300g), fish/meat/eggs, and sugar.</li>
          <li><strong>Clothing Norms:</strong> An annual consumption of <strong>72 yards of fabric</strong> per household of four, accommodating clothing, seasonal wear, and domestic linens.</li>
          <li><strong>Shelter & Housing Allocation:</strong> Evaluated at exactly <strong>10%</strong> of the combined food and clothing expenditures to support modest residential accommodations.</li>
          <li><strong>Fuel, Lighting & Miscellaneous:</strong> Evaluated at <strong>20%</strong> of the baseline subsistence outlay.</li>
          <li><strong>The Reptakos Brett Supreme Court Addition (25%):</strong> In its landmark 1991 judgment in <em>Workmen of Reptakos Brett & Co. Ltd. vs Management</em>, the Supreme Court of India mandated that an additional <strong>25%</strong> of the total minimum wage must be added to provide for children's education, medical requirements, domestic contingencies, festivals, and old-age social security.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

