      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>The 8th CPC Minimum Pay Scenarios: ₹34,560 vs ₹41,040 vs ₹51,480</h2>
        <p>
          The determination of the 8th Pay Commission starting floor centers on three economic models currently under evaluation:
        </p>

        <div class="comparison-grid">
          <div class="comparison-card floor">
            <h4>Scenario A: Conservative Floor (1.92x)</h4>
            <div class="comp-value">₹34,560 / month</div>
            <p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Represents an inflation-neutral model designed purely to absorb accrued 53% Dearness Allowance into basic pay while providing a modest real wage increment of ~25%. Protects baseline purchasing power without placing immediate strain on the union fiscal deficit.</p>
            <span class="comp-note">Considered the absolute lower boundary for pay revision.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="comparison-card working">
            <h4>Scenario B: NC-JCM Expected Working Base (2.28x)</h4>
            <div class="comp-value">₹41,040 / month</div>
            <p><strong>Rationale:</strong> The widely cited recommendation presented by Joint Consultative Machinery staff federations. Reflects current retail prices for cereals, pulses, housing rent, and medical outlays under the Aykroyd criteria, establishing an equitable balance between real wage growth and fiscal sustainability.</p>
            <span class="comp-note">Most probable benchmark for Union Cabinet approval.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="comparison-card demand">
            <h4>Scenario C: Staff Side Maximum Demand (2.86x)</h4>
            <div class="comp-value">₹51,480 / month</div>
            <p><strong>Rationale:</strong> The peak demand submitted by railway, defence, and postal federations. Argues that entry-level government staff require wage parity with private sector urban living standards to attract and retain technical competence in modern governance.</p>
            <span class="comp-note">Active bargaining benchmark for employee unions.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Impact on Minimum Pension: The 50% Rule Parity</h2>
        <p>
          The determination of Level 1 minimum basic pay carries immediate legal consequences for superannuated retirees and family pensioners.
        </p>
        <p>
          Under the provisions of the <strong>Central Civil Services (Pension) Rules, 2021</strong>, the minimum basic pension payable to any retiring government employee is legally fixed at exactly <strong>50% of the minimum basic pay</strong>:
        </p>
        <div class="callout-box">
          <h4>The 50% Minimum Pension Equation:</h4>
          <p class="formula-box">
            <strong>Statutory Minimum Basic Pension</strong> = 50% &times; Minimum Basic Pay (Level 1, Cell 1)
          </p>
          <ul class="editorial-list">
            <li><strong>7th CPC Baseline:</strong> 50% of ₹18,000 = <strong>₹9,000 per month</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>Under Scenario A (1.92x):</strong> 50% of ₹34,560 = <strong>₹17,280 per month</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>Under Scenario B (2.28x Working Base):</strong> 50% of ₹41,040 = <strong>₹20,520 per month</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>Under Scenario C (2.86x Maximum Demand):</strong> 50% of ₹51,480 = <strong>₹25,740 per month</strong>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Consequently, an upward adjustment in the Level 1 wage floor automatically doubles the statutory safety net for more than 67 lakh central and defence family pensioners.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

