      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Compression Ratio Analysis: Balancing Hierarchy and Equality</h2>
        <p>
          A vital consideration evaluated by Pay Commission economists is the <strong>Compression Ratio</strong>—the mathematical ratio between the minimum basic pay (Level 1, Cell 1) and the maximum basic pay (Apex Level 18, Cabinet Secretary of India).
        </p>
        <p>
          While the 1st Pay Commission maintained a wide compression ratio of 1 : 36.4, successive commissions compressed this spread to foster internal administrative cohesion. The 7th CPC established a ratio of <strong>1 : 13.9</strong> (₹18,000 to ₹2,50,000). 
        </p>
        <p>
          If the 8th Pay Commission preserves this established 1 : 13.9 compression spread, setting the Level 1 floor at <strong>₹41,040</strong> will naturally cap Apex Level 18 compensation at approximately <strong>₹5,70,000 per month</strong>, maintaining proportional hierarchy across all civil and defence cadres.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Impact on State Government Employees (Tamil Nadu, UP, Maharashtra & Beyond)</h2>
        <p>
          While the Central Government mandates minimum basic pay for Central Civil Servants, Defence personnel, Posts, and Railways, state administrations across <strong>Tamil Nadu</strong>, <strong>Uttar Pradesh</strong>, <strong>Maharashtra</strong>, <strong>Rajasthan</strong>, <strong>Bihar</strong>, and <strong>Karnataka</strong> adapt these structures through their respective State Pay Revision Committees.
        </p>
        <p>
